{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CORRECTION (the previous comment was posted from an unquoted shell context and had its backticked\ncode fragments STRIPPED — three of the four load-bearing details were silently lost. This is the\ncomplete version; disregard the mangled one above.)\n\nMy \"ONE funnel\" keystone claim was WRONG. 172e's review caught it with three independent findings,\nand I verified each myself before rewriting.\n\nWHAT I GOT WRONG: I said class names would be resolved at config.resolve_model because every step\nmodel passes through it. Three things are false about that.\n\n1. config.py CANNOT call model_classes at module level. model_classes.py imports FROM config.py and\n   its own module docstring -- which I wrote one ticket earlier -- states that config.py must never\n   import it back. I proposed the exact cycle I had just prohibited.\n\n2. model_ladder BYPASSES resolve_model entirely. VERIFIED in source:\n       production_batch_runner.py:93   cfg = dataclasses.replace(cfg, model=req.model_ladder[0])\n       code_review/batch_runner.py:55  model = req.model_ladder[0] if req.model_ladder else None\n   Ladder rungs travel through BatchRunRequest straight to the batch runners and never touch\n   resolve_model. So a class name in a ladder would have reached the runner UNRESOLVED.\n\n3. The verifier paths bypass it too. resolve_verifier_model (verify.py:269) is its own path, and\n   _verifier_cfg (plan_review/__init__.py:46) is a further consumer with TWO call sites at\n   xcheck.py:198 and xcheck.py:336 that no plan had enumerated.\n\nCORRECTED MECHANISM -- one FUNCTION, three CALL SITES (not one funnel):\n\n    model_classes.resolve_model_string(value: str) -> str\n        if value in (\"trivial\", \"standard\", \"frontier\"):  resolve via load_class_slots()\n        else:                                             return value unchanged\n\nCalled at exactly three places:\n  a. config.resolve_model      -- via a LAZY, function-level import. That creates no module-level\n                                  cycle, and it is already the established pattern in this very\n                                  file: config.py:108, :566 and :768 all do function-level imports.\n  b. the ladder expansion      -- production_batch_runner.py:93 and code_review/batch_runner.py:55\n  c. the verifier path         -- resolve_verifier_model, plus _verifier_cfg and its xcheck callers\n\nModule-scope import direction is UNCHANGED and still one-way: model_classes imports config normally;\nconfig imports model_classes only inside a function body.\n\nWHY ONE FUNCTION STILL MATTERS even though there is no single funnel: the reserved-word vocabulary\nand the class-to-id resolution live in exactly one place, so the three call sites cannot drift apart.\nWhat I gave up is the false claim that a single interception point exists. Pretending it did would\nhave shipped a change where the step path resolved classes correctly and its tests passed, while\nladders and verifiers silently passed the literal strings \"standard\"/\"frontier\" through to a\nprovider as if they were model ids.\n\nThe USER-FACING syntax is unchanged. In YAML, model: standard and\nmodel_ladder: [trivial, standard, frontier] still work exactly as decided on this ticket; only the\ninternal wiring is corrected."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785498553401071001,"uuid":"e01232e1-191d-4ea0-aab0-505378725d75"}